Therefore, $ P = \frac{1}{a \times b} $.

So, if I can find the value of $ a \times b $, then I can take its reciprocal to find P.

So, let me compute $ a \times b $ first.

Given that:

$ a = \sqrt[3]{6} = 6^{1/3} $

and

$ b = \sqrt[3]{\frac{1}{162}} = \left( \frac{1}{162} \right)^{1/3} $

Therefore, $ a \times b = 6^{1/3} \times \left( \frac{1}{162} \right)^{1/3} $

Now, I can combine these two terms because they are both raised to the power of 1/3, so I can combine the exponents.

Recall that $ x^{m} \times y^{n} = (x^{m} \times y^{n}) $, but here both terms are raised to the same exponent, which is 1/3, so maybe I can combine them as follows:

